# Backlog — how to write a work item

This folder is the **unified work backlog** for this vision: the **features** and **bugfixes**
that attempt to move the bets, plus standalone work that serves the product. A backlog item is
a unit of work with **no hypothesis and no metric** — if it has a metric to move, it's a
**bet**, not a backlog item (see the home `AGENTS.md`).

Read this before adding an item. Per-type detail (how to phrase a feature vs a bug) lives in
the subfolder `AGENTS.md`.

## Subfolders — pick one
- **`feature/`** — a new user-facing capability. → see `feature/AGENTS.md`.
- **`bugfix/`** — broken behavior you can reproduce, or at minimum describe (expected vs
  actual). → see `bugfix/AGENTS.md`.

One file per item: `backlog/<type>/<slug>.md`. The filename slug is the item's id and its
`[[wikilink]]` target — keep it short and stable.

## Frontmatter (every item)
```yaml
---
type: feature            # feature | bugfix — matches the subfolder
status: backlog          # backlog → prd-drafted → building → shipped
bet: "[[<bet-slug>]]"    # optional — the bet this serves; omit if standalone
repos: [<repo>]          # repo(s) the work will likely land in
created: <YYYY-MM-DD>
prd:                     # set by /intent on register-back, e.g. "<repo>#<branch> (prds/<feature>)"
---
```
- **`status`** lifecycle: `backlog` (just filed) → `prd-drafted` (`/intent` filed a PRD) →
  `building` (on a feature branch) → `shipped` (merged). Only `/intent` and the human keep
  this current; the headless harness does not write here.
- **`bet`** is the output→outcome link. Set it when the item serves a bet; leave it off for a
  pure bug or unaligned capability. An item serves at most one bet — if it serves two, it's
  probably two items.

## Don't maintain an index — query
To see everything serving a bet, query the frontmatter (in `roadmap.md` or anywhere):
~~~
```dataview
TABLE type, status, prd FROM "<vision>/backlog"
WHERE bet = [[<bet-slug>]]
SORT status ASC
```
~~~

## The altitude line (don't cross it)
A backlog item describes **the problem and why it matters** — never the solution. The solution
is the PRD's job (`/intent` slices the item into one or more `prds/<feature>/`, each with a
runnable definition of done). Keep the item outcome-shaped; let the PRD be solution-shaped.
